WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.657
This query places two LAST_VALUE calculations side by side.

00:00:03.657 --> 00:00:06.102
The first uses the default ordered frame.

00:00:06.102 --> 00:00:10.637
The second explicitly extends from unbounded preceding to unbounded following.

00:00:10.637 --> 00:00:13.002
Run the query and compare the columns.

00:00:13.002 --> 00:00:17.407
This example shows why frame boundaries are part of the meaning of a window function.

00:00:17.407 --> 00:00:21.877
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:27.377
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:29.432
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:34.337
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:39.952
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:39.952 --> 00:00:41.177
before discussing syntax.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:46.710
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
